**VERDICT: REVISE**

**Overall Assessment**: 
The `TabsWidget` implements foundational ARIA markup (`role="tablist"`, `role="tab"`, `role="tabpanel"`, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls`, `aria-labelledby`) and visual styles, but breaks the essential interaction model defined by the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Tabs pattern. Because roving tabindex (`tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}`) is used without implementing arrow key navigation (ArrowLeft, ArrowRight, Home, End), keyboard users who focus the tablist cannot reach or activate any tab other than the initially active one. Additionally, the tabpanels lack `tabIndex={0}` or interactive focusable elements, preventing keyboard-only users from reaching panel content without relying on mouse input.

---

### Pre-commitment Predictions
- **Predicted**: Incomplete WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern (missing ArrowLeft/ArrowRight navigation, missing Home/End key support).
- **Predicted**: Focus trapping/reachability breakdown when roving `tabindex="-1"` is paired with no arrow key listener.
- **Predicted**: Missing `tabIndex={0}` on `role="tabpanel"` leaving static panel text unreachable by keyboard Tab order.
- **Predicted**: ID mismatch bug in Enter/Space key handler (`e.currentTarget.id` vs `t.id`).
- **Actual**: All predicted gaps are present in the component implementation.

---

### Critical Findings (blocks access)

1. **Roving tabindex without arrow key handlers renders inactive tabs unreachable via keyboard**
   - **Code Reference**: `TabsWidget.jsx:10-15, 27`
   - **Element**: `button[role="tab"]` inside `[role="tablist"]`
   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users and screen reader users navigating in virtual PC/browse mode.
   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern (Keyboard Interaction).
   - **Why this matters**: Inactive tabs have `tabIndex={-1}`, taking them out of the standard Tab ring. Because `handleTabKeyDown` only intercepts `Enter` and `' '`, pressing `Tab` moves focus completely out of the tablist past the component. A keyboard user has no mechanism to move focus to or activate tabs 1..n.
   - **Confidence**: HIGH
   - **Fix**: Implement APG arrow key navigation (`ArrowRight`, `ArrowLeft`, `Home`, `End`) to calculate next/previous/first/last tab index, update `activeTab`, and explicitly move DOM focus (`tabRefs.current[nextIndex].focus()`).

2. **Inactive tabpanel content is unreachable via keyboard when panel contains static text**
   - **Code Reference**: `TabsWidget.jsx:36-47`
   - **Element**: `div[role="tabpanel"]`
   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users.
   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern.
   - **Why this matters**: The tabpanel renders plain `<p>{tab.content}</p>` with no interactive elements and lacks `tabIndex={0}`. Once a keyboard user activates a tab, pressing `Tab` jumps completely over the panel content to the next focusable element on the page. Per APG, if a tabpanel does not contain a focusable child element, the panel container itself must have `tabIndex={0}` so keyboard users can scroll/read it.
   - **Confidence**: HIGH
   - **Fix**: Add `tabIndex={0}` to each `role="tabpanel"` container (e.g., `<div role="tabpanel" tabIndex={0} ...>`).

---

### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)

1. **ID mismatch in keydown handler breaks Enter/Space activation**
   - **Code Reference**: `TabsWidget.jsx:10-15, 23`
   - **Element**: `onKeyDown={handleTabKeyDown}`
   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users and screen reader users.
   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A).
   - **Why this matters**: In `handleTabKeyDown`, the lookup is `tabs.findIndex(t => t.id === e.currentTarget.id)`, but the button's DOM ID is rendered as `id={`tab-${tab.id}`}`. `t.id === `tab-${tab.id}`` evaluates to `false`, returning `-1`. Passing `-1` to `setActiveTab` breaks the active state, hiding all panels and breaking `aria-selected` tracking entirely.
   - **Confidence**: HIGH
   - **Fix**: Pass the tab index directly to the handler or match against `tab-${t.id}`:
     ```jsx
     const handleTabKeyDown = (e, index) => {
       // use index directly instead of DOM ID string matching
     };
     ```

2. **Inactive tab button text contrast fails WCAG 1.4.3 minimum threshold**
   - **Code Reference**: `TabsWidget.css:15` (`color: #666` on `#ffffff` background)
   - **Element**: `.tab-button` (inactive state)
   - **User Group Impacted**: Low vision users and users in bright environments.
   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) (Level AA).
   - **Why this matters**: `#666666` text against a `#ffffff` background yields a contrast ratio of **4.48:1**, which is strictly below the required **4.5:1** ratio for normal (16px, regular weight) body text.
   - **Confidence**: HIGH
   - **Fix**: Darken inactive text color to at least `#595959` (7.0:1) or `#5c5c5c` (4.58:1).

---

### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)

1. **Missing `aria-orientation` declaration on `role="tablist"`**
   - **Code Reference**: `TabsWidget.jsx:19`
   - **Element**: `<div role="tablist" aria-label="Content tabs">`
   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern.
   - **Why this matters**: While `aria-orientation="horizontal"` is the default implicit value for `tablist`, declaring `aria-orientation="horizontal"` explicitly clarifies the intended key navigation model (Left/Right arrows vs Up/Down arrows) to all assistive technology.
   - **Fix**: Add `aria-orientation="horizontal"` to the tablist.

---

### Enhancements (best practice not met)

1. **Support both automatic and manual selection modes**:
   - For tabs where content loading is lightweight, moving focus with Arrow keys should automatically select the tab (Automatic Activation). For tabs where panel rendering causes heavy DOM updates or data fetching, moving focus with Arrow keys should decouple from selection until Space/Enter is pressed (Manual Activation).

---

### What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)

- **Missing Arrow, Home, End key handlers**: The keyboard navigation loop is completely missing.
- **Missing DOM Focus Management / Ref handling**: Even if `activeTab` state updates via arrow keys, DOM focus does not move to the newly active tab button without `useRef` array bindings.
- **Missing focusable container for panel content**: Static panel text cannot be tabbed into.

---

### Perspective Alarm Levels

| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |
|-------------|-------------|----------------|
| Screen reader | **MEDIUM** | ARIA markup is present, but keyboard navigation failure impairs virtual cursor & focus transitions |
| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Inactive tabs are unreachable; static panel content is skipped |
| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Inactive tab text fails 4.5:1 contrast requirement (#666 on white = 4.48:1) |
| Cognitive | **LOW** | Clear label and simple tab structure |
| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | Only minimal color transition (`0.2s`) present |
| Auditory access | **LOW** | No multimedia or audio signals |
| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | Inactive text contrast boundary failure |

---

### Multi-Perspective Notes

- **Screen reader user**: Screen readers announce "tab 1 of N, selected", but upon trying to navigate between tabs using widget keys or Tab, the user will be dumped out of the tablist because tabs 2..N have `tabindex="-1"` and arrow keys do nothing.
- **Keyboard-only user**: Completely locked out of tabs 2 through N. `Tab` bypasses them; arrow keys produce default browser scroll behavior; Enter/Space with the ID bug breaks active tab selection.
- **Low vision user**: Inactive tabs at `#666` are washed out on white screens, making it difficult to perceive unselected options. Focus ring on the active button is visible (3px solid `#0066cc`), which is good.
- **Cognitive user**: Simple mental model, but confusing when pressing standard arrow keys fails to change or inspect other tabs.

---

### Verdict Justification

The component earns a **REVISE** verdict. While the developer correctly intended to use the ARIA Tabs pattern, introducing `tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}` without the accompanying arrow key event handlers created an access barrier: keyboard users cannot access any tab except the first one.

---

### Recommended Remediation Code

```jsx
import React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

const TabsWidget = ({ tabs }) => {
  const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState(0);
  const tabRefs = useRef([]);

  const focusTab = (index) => {
    setActiveTab(index);
    tabRefs.current[index]?.focus();
  };

  const handleTabKeyDown = (e, index) => {
    const count = tabs.length;
    let nextIndex = null;

    switch (e.key) {
      case 'ArrowRight':
        nextIndex = (index + 1) % count;
        break;
      case 'ArrowLeft':
        nextIndex = (index - 1 + count) % count;
        break;
      case 'Home':
        nextIndex = 0;
        break;
      case 'End':
        nextIndex = count - 1;
        break;
      default:
        return;
    }

    e.preventDefault();
    focusTab(nextIndex);
  };

  return (
    <div className="tabs-container">
      <div 
        role="tablist" 
        aria-label="Content tabs" 
        aria-orientation="horizontal"
      >
        {tabs.map((tab, index) => (
          <button
            key={tab.id}
            ref={(el) => (tabRefs.current[index] = el)}
            id={`tab-${tab.id}`}
            role="tab"
            aria-selected={index === activeTab}
            aria-controls={`panel-${tab.id}`}
            tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}
            onClick={() => setActiveTab(index)}
            onKeyDown={(e) => handleTabKeyDown(e, index)}
            className={`tab-button ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}
          >
            {tab.label}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div className="tab-panels">
        {tabs.map((tab, index) => (
          <div
            key={tab.id}
            id={`panel-${tab.id}`}
            role="tabpanel"
            aria-labelledby={`tab-${tab.id}`}
            tabIndex={0}
            hidden={index !== activeTab}
            className={`tab-panel ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}
          >
            <p>{tab.content}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TabsWidget;
```
